anguage of the interval Hammersley process, k,eﬀ LH,IN T , is the set of strings in Σk∗ obtained by deleting all diamonds from some string in LkH,IN T .Increments of w are those positions where the insertion of a k does not remove any life- line, thus increasing the number of heaps in the corresponding greedy “patience heaping” algorithm [9] by 1. If w has t increment positions then the probability that the number of heaps will increase by one (given that the current state of the process is w) is t/(n + 1). What we need to show is that (as n → ∞) the mean number of positions that are increments in a random sample w of length n tends to λk . Therefore the probability that a new position will increase the number of heaps by 1 is asymptotically equal to λk /(n + 1). The scaling of the expected number of heaps follows from this limit. The Language (and Series) of Hammersley-Type Processes 85 Fig. 6. Probability distribution of increments, for k = 2, and n = 5, 9, 13, 1000000. n 2 3 4 5 6 7 E[#inc2 ] 1.0 1.166 1.208 1.250 1.281 1.307 n 8 9 10 100 100000 1 mil E[#inc2 ] 1.329 1.347 1.363 1.520 1.575 1.580 Fig. 7. The mean values of the distributions of increments. In Fig. 6, we plot the exact probability distribution of the number of incre- ments (from which we subtract one, to make the distribution start from zero) for k = 2 and several small values of n. They were computed exactly by employ- ing Algorithm 3 to exactly compute the probability of each string w, and then computing #inc2 (w). We performed this computation for 2 ≤ n ≤ 13. The corresponding expected values are tabulated (for all values n = 2, . . . , 10) in Fig. 7. Unfortunately, as it turns out, the ability to exactly compute (for small values of n) the distribution of increments does not give an accurate esti- mate of the asymptotic behavior of this distribution, as the convergence seems rather slow, and not at all captured by these small values of n. Indeed, to explore the distribution of increments for large values of n, as exact compu- tation is no longer possible, we instead resorted to sampling from the distri- bution, by generating 10000 independent random trajectories of length n from process HAD2 , and then computing the distribution of increments of the sam- pled outcome strings. The outcome is presented (for n = 100, 100000, 1000000, together with some of the cases of the exact distribution) in Fig. 6. The distri- bution of increments seems √ to converge (as n → ∞) to a geometric distribution with parameter p = 5−1 2 ∼ 0.618 · · · . That is, we predict that for all i ≥ 1, limn→∞ P r|w|=n [#inc2 (w) = i] = p · (1 − p)i−1 . The ﬁt between the (sampled) estimates for n = 1000000 and the predicted limit distribution is quite good: every coeﬃcient diﬀers from its predicted value by no more than 0.003, with the exception of the fourth coeﬃcient, whose diﬀerence is 0.007.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiscrete Mathematics and Theoretical Computer Science (https://dmtcs.episciences.org/6540/pdf)\nciteturn0search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6.4 years ago; On the Heapability of Finite Partial Orders ... According to this conjecture, just as in the case of random permutations, the scaling behavior of the ... hand, somewhat surprisingly, the scaling behavior of sets of random intervals seems to be similar to that ... Probability theory and related ﬁelds, 103(2):199–213, 1995. ... puter Science, Carnegie Mellon University, Nov.\nOn the Heapability of Finite Partial Orders\n21\nConjecture 1. For every k ≥2 there exists a positive constant ck > 0 such that, if Rn is a sequence of n\nrandom intervals then\nlim\nn→∞\nE[k-wd(Rn)]\nn\n= ck\n(4)\nMoreover ck =\n1\nk+1.\nAccording to this conjecture, just as in the case of random permutations, the scaling behavior of the\nk-width changes when going from k = 1 to k = 2. Note, though, that the direction of change is different\n(Θ(√n) to Θ(log n) for integer sequences, Θ(√n) to 